    1. resource_efficiency (no throughput floor)
       Q: Is there a minimum production quantity or revenue target this shift must meet, independent of efficiency? Without one, the optimiser can maximise value-per-resource-unit by drastically cutting volume (e.g., producing only a handful of high-margin units), which may satisfy the ratio while missing delivery commitments — please specify a minimum good-units or throughput value floor.
       A: Apply a throughput floor equal to 90% of the historical average shift output for the same product mix; if no historical baseline is available, set floor to the confirmed customer order quantity for the shift.
    2. priority_order vs. _raw_priority_order conflict
       Q: The compiled contract lists only 'HumanWellbeing' in priority_order, implying fatigue and noise dominate everything, but the raw intent order places resource_efficiency first — which governs when fatigue is currently at 0.39 (just under the hard cap) and running one more cycle would breach 0.40: does the optimiser stop to protect the operator, or push through to maximise efficiency?
       A: Hard constraints on fatigue (≤ 0.4) and noise (≤ 80 dB) are inviolable and always take precedence over resource_efficiency gains; the priority_order field as compiled ('HumanWellbeing' first) is authoritative.
    3. resource_efficiency denominator (multi-resource aggregation weight)
       Q: Resource consumption covers energy, direct labour hours, and direct materials — what relative weights should be used to aggregate them into one 'resource unit'? For example, if the optimiser can trade 20% more materials for 10% less labour and 5% less energy, is that a net resource saving or a net cost increase — please supply a cost-per-unit weight for each resource type, or confirm that monetary cost is the aggregation basis.
       A: Use current standard cost rates (£/kWh for energy, £/labour-hour, £/material-unit) as aggregation weights; if cost data is unavailable, weight energy at 40%, labour at 40%, and materials at 20% of the composite denominator.
    4. operator_fatigue threshold calibration vs. shift duration
       Q: The fatigue cap is set at 0.4 (on a 0–1 normalised index), but the index calibration is assumed for a standard 8-hour shift — if tonight's shift runs 10 or 12 hours (common in 4-on/4-off rosters), does the 0.4 limit still apply, or should it be scaled down proportionally (e.g., 0.30 for a 12-hour shift) to maintain the same physiological protection?
       A: Fatigue cap remains 0.4 for any shift ≤ 8 hours; for each additional hour beyond 8, the cap is reduced by 0.025 (e.g., 0.35 for 10 h, 0.30 for 12 h) unless the manager specifies otherwise.
    5. noise_level measurement scope (LAeq vs. peak)
       Q: The 80 dB limit is measured as LAeq (continuous A-weighted average at operator ear level), but a brief machine spike to 95 dB averaged over a 15-minute window can still comply — should there also be a peak (LCpeak or LAmax) limit, for example no instantaneous exceedance above 85 dB, to prevent acute hearing damage even when the shift average stays under 80 dB?
       A: Enforce only the LAeq ≤ 80 dB constraint as written; no separate peak limit is applied unless the manager adds one, consistent with the ISO 9612 measurement basis stated in the contract assumptions.

