  [L1] Ambiguity detection: 5 issue(s) found
    1. priority_order vs context._raw_priority_order
       Q: Should resource_efficiency be maximised first and human-wellbeing constraints treated as guardrails (raw priority order: efficiency → fatigue → noise), or should fatigue and noise be satisfied first with efficiency optimised only inside that feasible region (compiled priority: HumanWellbeing first) — the two orderings produce materially different schedules when the efficiency-maximising solution sits near the fatigue boundary?
       A: HumanWellbeing is treated as lexicographically dominant: fatigue ≤ 0.4 and noise ≤ 80 dB are satisfied before any resource_efficiency optimisation is attempted, consistent with the compiled priority_order field.
    2. resource_efficiency (maximize, threshold: null)
       Q: Is there a maximum total resource budget per shift (e.g. an energy ceiling in kWh or a raw-material cap in kg) beyond which additional efficiency gains should not be pursued — given that uncapped maximisation can drive energy consumption up ~40 % above baseline when machine speeds are pushed to their limits?
       A: No explicit resource ceiling is imposed; the optimiser may increase absolute resource consumption provided the fatigue and noise hard constraints remain satisfied.
    3. operator_fatigue (threshold 0.4, linear accumulation model)
       Q: Is the 0.4 fatigue limit a ceiling that must never be crossed at any instant during the shift, or an end-of-shift accumulated value — because a linear model allows peak-intensity task bursts to push instantaneous fatigue above 0.4 mid-shift while the shift-end value still lands at or below the threshold?
       A: The 0.4 limit is enforced as an end-of-shift accumulated value; instantaneous intra-shift spikes are not separately constrained unless a real-time fatigue sensor feed is available.
    4. resource_scope (labor_hours) vs operator_task_allocation actuation target
       Q: If reallocating tasks to fewer operators reduces total labour-hours and thereby raises resource_efficiency, is there a minimum crew size per shift that must be maintained regardless of efficiency gains — since the optimiser could legally assign all tasks to a single operator if no staffing floor is specified?
       A: The minimum operator count is fixed at the current baseline crew size for the shift; the optimiser may reassign tasks across that crew but may not reduce headcount below the baseline.
    5. noise_level (80 dB, simultaneous multi-machine operation)
       Q: When equipment_selection causes two or more machines to run concurrently and their combined sound pressure at a workstation exceeds 80 dB (co-located sources add roughly 3 dB per doubling), should the constraint be evaluated at the single worst-case operator workstation or as an area-averaged figure — because the distinction changes which equipment combinations are feasible?
       A: The 80 dB limit is evaluated at the single loudest occupied operator workstation; combined noise from simultaneously active equipment is computed at that worst-case measurement point using logarithmic addition of individual source levels.

