  [Escalation] Remediation options presented to manager:
    - Option 1 (Recommended): Extend Horizon via Additional Shift or Workforce Expansion: Absorb the 25% demand increase by adding a supplementary shift crew or temporary workforce augmentation, distributing workload so that neither FatigueIndex nor Noise limits are breached for any individual worker group. This is the safest approach and fully preserves all human-centric constraints. It may require lead time to staff and will incur additional labor cost, but avoids regulatory risk and protects worker wellbeing. Expected production completion will slip by approximately 20–25% relative to original deadline unless additional resources are onboarded within the planning window.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 2 (Moderate Risk): Noise Limit Temporary Relaxation with Mandatory PPE Protocol: Relax the Noise constraint from 80.0 dB to 85.0 dB for the duration of the elevated-demand period, contingent on mandatory use of rated hearing protection (HPDs achieving ≥10 dB attenuation) by all workers in affected zones, enforced shift rotation (max 4-hour continuous exposure per worker), and an audiometric monitoring checkpoint within 7 days. FatigueIndex must remain at or below 0.40 — fatigue management via job rotation and break scheduling is required. This option partially closes the deadline gap while maintaining the fatigue constraint in full. The noise relaxation carries a residual occupational health risk if PPE compliance is not enforced, and must be documented as a formal constraint waiver.
      Preserves human constraints: No
    - Option 3 (High Risk, Last Resort): Dual Constraint Temporary Relaxation with Enhanced Monitoring: Temporarily relax both FatigueIndex (from 0.40 to 0.65) and Noise (from 80.0 dB to 85.0 dB) to meet the full demand within the original deadline. This option must be paired with: mandatory PPE for noise, enforced rest periods (minimum 11 hours between shifts), a daily fatigue self-assessment protocol, a medical standby contact on-call, and automatic revert to original limits after 72 hours or upon demand normalization. This option carries meaningful regulatory and liability exposure — it should only be authorized if there is a contractual or operational imperative that outweighs the health risk, and must be formally signed off by both the production manager and the site health and safety officer. All deviations must be logged in the CBPA audit trail.
      Preserves human constraints: No

  [Manager] Decision: Option C
    Rationale: Prioritizing human well-being over deadline
