  [Escalation] Remediation options presented to manager:
    - Option 1 — Expand Shift Capacity with Additional Workforce (Recommended): Introduce one additional parallel shift or hire temporary operators to distribute the incremental 15% demand across a larger workforce. Fatigue accumulation per worker drops proportionally as workload is shared, and noise exposure windows per individual shorten. This approach targets the root cause of both violations without touching constraint thresholds. Lead time of 1–3 days is required for scheduling and safety onboarding. Expected production shortfall during transition is approximately 5%.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 2 — Engineering Controls Plus Ergonomic Rotation Protocol: Deploy acoustic dampening panels and anti-vibration mounts at high-noise workstations (targeting a 4–6 dB reduction, bringing noise to ~79–81 dB), and implement a structured operator rotation schedule that caps continuous exposure per worker to reduce FatigueIndex accumulation. Modeling suggests noise can be brought within limit; fatigue improvement depends on rotation frequency and may require a 10–12% demand deferral to a subsequent planning window. No constraint thresholds are formally altered. Implementation lag is estimated at 2–5 business days for physical installation.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 3 — Temporary Supervised Constraint Relaxation with Compensatory Safeguards (Requires Manager Sign-Off): With explicit documented authorization, temporarily raise the FatigueIndex ceiling to 0.50 (from 0.40) and the Noise ceiling to 83.0 dB (from 80.0 dB) for a defined short window (not to exceed 5 consecutive shifts). Mandatory compensatory measures must accompany this relaxation: (a) mandatory PPE upgrade to EN 352-rated hearing protection for all floor operators, (b) 15-minute additional rest breaks every 90 minutes, (c) real-time fatigue monitoring with auto-halt triggers at 0.55, and (d) post-window occupational health review. This option meets the demand surge deadline at near-zero gap but does not fully preserve the original human constraint thresholds and must be logged in the governance audit trail.
