  [L1] Ambiguity detection: 5 issue(s) found
    1. resource_efficiency (aggregation weights)
       Q: How should labor hours, energy (kWh), and raw material throughput be combined into a single resource denominator — equal normalized weights, cost-based weights, or a primary resource with the others as side-constraints? Without explicit weights the optimiser may increase energy consumption by ~40% to lift delivered units while technically respecting the fatigue and noise limits.
       A: Apply cost-proportional weights derived from the prior-shift cost ledger (labour, energy, material in monetary units); re-normalize so weights sum to 1.0 and freeze them at shift start.
    2. resource_efficiency (unbounded maximization vs. satisficing target)
       Q: Is the intent to maximize resource efficiency without limit, or to hit a specific improvement target (e.g., ≥ 5 % above the rolling baseline)? Unbounded maximization will push the optimiser to hold operator fatigue at 0.399 and noise at 79.9 dB continuously, leaving no safety margin for measurement error or transient spikes.
       A: Convert to a satisficing target of baseline + 8 %; once met, the optimiser minimizes fatigue rather than continuing to push efficiency, preserving a fatigue headroom of ≥ 0.05 below the hard limit.
    3. operator_fatigue_index (continuous enforcement vs. end-of-shift snapshot)
       Q: Does the fatigue ≤ 0.4 constraint apply at every scheduling time-step throughout the shift (requiring the optimiser to model operator rotation and rest breaks), or only as a snapshot at shift end? This choice changes feasibility: continuous enforcement requires knowing how many substitute operators are available.
       A: Enforce continuously at each 30-minute scheduling interval; trigger a mandatory 10-minute rest or operator rotation when the predicted index reaches 0.35 (early-warning buffer = 0.05), assuming at least one qualified substitute operator is available per station.
    4. ambient_noise_level (L_eq shift-average vs. instantaneous peak)
       Q: The 80 dB limit is defined as L_eq (continuous equivalent, shift-average). Should the 80 dB ceiling also apply to instantaneous peaks (L_Amax), or only to the average? A schedule that clusters high-noise operations can satisfy the L_eq limit while producing short bursts above 85 dB, which are independently hazardous under OSHA/EU impulsive-noise rules.
       A: Enforce 80 dB on L_eq as the primary hard constraint; additionally flag any predicted L_Amax > 85 dB as a soft warning that surfaces in the solution report but does not render the schedule infeasible.
    5. resource_efficiency_baseline (rolling-average window and outlier handling)
       Q: The baseline is described as a 'prior-shift rolling average,' but the window length and treatment of anomalous shifts (e.g., a shift with > 20 % unplanned downtime) are unspecified. A single bad shift can deflate the baseline, making even a small improvement look large, or inflate it, making the target unachievable. How many prior shifts should the window span, and should outliers be excluded?
       A: Use a 5-shift trimmed mean (drop the single highest and single lowest values); exclude any shift where recorded downtime exceeded 20 % of shift duration; if fewer than 3 valid shifts exist, fall back to the site-wide monthly average for the same product mix.

