    1. value_efficiency / context.resource_types
       Q: When computing value_units/resource_unit, how should the four resource types (labor, energy, materials, machine_time) be combined into a single denominator — by their real-time monetary cost, by equal unit weighting, or by a fixed physical aggregate? A 10 % difference in the weighting formula can shift the optimizer's preferred schedule by ~30–40 %.
       A: Aggregate resource consumption using standard monetary unit costs (e.g., $/operator-hr for labor, $/kWh for energy, $/kg for materials, $/machine-hr for amortized machine time) summed at market rates. This will be revisited if cost data is unavailable.
    2. operator_fatigue_ceiling (hard_constraints.limit = 0.4)
       Q: Is the 0.4 fatigue ceiling enforced as a never-exceed instantaneous limit at every measurement interval, or as the allowable peak cumulative value at shift end? If instantaneous, can early-shift intensity bursts above 0.4 be tolerated if followed by recovery periods that bring the cumulative index back down?
       A: Treat 0.4 as a hard instantaneous ceiling: no single measurement interval may exceed 0.4 regardless of prior or subsequent fatigue levels. No burst-and-recover pattern is permitted.
    3. noise_ceiling (hard_constraints.limit = 80.0 dB) vs context.assumptions.noise_measurement = 'A_weighted_SPL_dBA_time_averaged'
       Q: The hard constraint says noise ≤ 80 dB, but the assumption specifies time-averaging — does 80 dB refer to the shift-long time-weighted average (allowing brief spikes well above 80 dB) or is it an instantaneous ceiling that must never be crossed at any moment? Misinterpreting this could expose operators to peak SPL exceeding 90–95 dB while the average stays compliant.
       A: Apply 80 dB as a rolling short-interval instantaneous ceiling (no single interval average may exceed 80 dB). Any interval breaching 85 dB triggers a corrective-action flag regardless of shift-long average.
    4. value_efficiency (kpi_targets.threshold = null)
       Q: There is no upper saturation target for value_efficiency: should the optimizer pursue maximum efficiency without limit, or is there a 'good enough' threshold (e.g., ≥ 20 % above shift baseline) beyond which further gains are treated as low-priority? Without a ceiling, the optimizer may accept large resource trade-offs for diminishing marginal gains.
       A: Apply a soft saturation target at 25 % above the baseline value_efficiency. Gains beyond this threshold are deprioritized relative to fatigue and noise minimization unless explicitly unlocked by the manager.
    5. context.assumptions.operator_count_fixed_during_shift / operator_fatigue_ceiling scope
       Q: With operator count fixed, can the optimizer reallocate individual operators to different tasks mid-shift (potentially raising one operator's fatigue while lowering another's), and is the 0.4 fatigue ceiling applied per individual operator or to the fleet-average? Fleet-averaging could permit one operator to reach fatigue 0.7 while another stays at 0.1.
       A: Apply the 0.4 fatigue ceiling strictly per individual operator. Task reallocation between operators is permitted only if no individual breaches the ceiling at any interval.

