       Q: The formal priority_order lists only 'HumanWellbeing', but _raw_priority_order places resource_efficiency first and fatigue second. When maximising efficiency would push the fatigue index from, say, 0.25 to exactly 0.39 (still under the hard limit), should the optimizer accept that trade-off, or is operator wellbeing strictly lexicographically dominant over efficiency gains?
       A: HumanWellbeing is treated as lexicographically dominant: the optimizer maximises resource_efficiency only within the subset of schedules that also minimise fatigue accumulation, accepting no increase in fatigue for an efficiency gain smaller than 5 %.
    2. resource_efficiency / minimum throughput floor
       Q: resource_efficiency is an unbounded ratio (value / resources). Without a production floor the optimizer could achieve a high ratio by running a single small, highly efficient micro-batch rather than filling the shift. Is there a minimum deliverable volume—e.g., at least 80 % of the standard shift quota of conforming units—that must be met regardless of the efficiency ratio?
       A: A soft lower bound of 75 % of the historical average shift throughput is enforced; any schedule that falls below this is penalised as infeasible even if its efficiency ratio is superior.
    3. ambient_noise_level / shift_duration
       Q: The 80 dB ceiling is calibrated to an 8-hour OSHA action level (LAeq,8h). If the actual shift runs 10 or 12 hours the permissible equivalent level drops to approximately 78 dB or 77 dB respectively to maintain the same cumulative dose. What is the scheduled shift length, and should the noise limit be recalculated automatically for shifts longer than 8 hours?
       A: Shift duration is assumed to be exactly 8 hours and the 80 dB ceiling is held fixed. If operational data later indicates a longer shift, the noise limit is recalculated as 80 − 10·log10(T/8) dB where T is the actual shift length in hours.
    4. operator_fatigue_index direction in kpi_targets
       Q: The original intent says 'keep fatigue low', which implies the optimizer should actively minimise fatigue—not merely keep it below 0.4. The current kpi_targets entry only sets an upper_bound of 0.4, so a schedule that holds fatigue at 0.39 throughout is treated as equivalent to one that holds it at 0.15. Should operator_fatigue_index be converted to a 'minimize' objective (with 0.4 as a hard ceiling), or is any value below 0.4 equally acceptable?
       A: operator_fatigue_index direction is changed from upper_bound to minimize, with 0.4 retained as the hard-constraint ceiling. The optimizer will prefer lower fatigue profiles among schedules with comparable efficiency.
    5. resource_efficiency / absolute resource component caps
       Q: The efficiency ratio (value / total resources) can increase even when absolute energy and material consumption rise, so long as output grows faster. Without per-component caps, the optimizer may raise energy draw by 30–40 % to unlock a 10 % efficiency improvement. Should any individual resource component (e.g., energy ≤ X kWh, raw material ≤ Y kg) carry an absolute ceiling independent of the efficiency ratio?
       A: Each resource component is capped at 110 % of its rolling 30-shift average consumption. Schedules that exceed this cap on any single component are rejected even if the aggregate efficiency ratio is higher.

