    3. operator_fatigue (constraint enforcement timing — continuous vs. end-of-shift)
       Q: Is the fatigue ≤ 0.4 constraint a continuous running limit enforced at every scheduling interval throughout the shift, or only at shift end — because an end-of-shift interpretation allows the optimiser to schedule a high-intensity opening block (fatigue peaking at ~0.6–0.7) followed by a recovery period, reaching ≤ 0.4 at close while violating ergonomic intent for hours?
       A: Fatigue is treated as a continuous constraint evaluated at every 30-minute interval; the score must remain ≤ 0.4 at all measurement points, not only at shift end.
    4. resource_efficiency_definition (labor hours in denominator — headcount flexibility)
       Q: Labor hours appear in the resource denominator, so reducing crew size or accelerating individual work pace both improve the ratio — is operator headcount fixed at the planned shift roster, or may the optimiser reallocate or reduce staffing, and if reallocation is allowed, what is the minimum safe staffing level per workstation?
       A: Operator headcount is fixed at the planned shift roster for this contract horizon; labor hours in the denominator are treated as a constant, making energy consumption and materials the only controllable resource terms the optimiser may adjust.
    5. noise_level (LAeq shift average vs. intra-shift peak exposure)
       Q: The 80 dB limit is defined as an LAeq shift average, which allows short bursts above 85–87 dB (EU Directive 2003/10/EC action and limit values) to be masked by quieter periods — should a supplementary short-term peak constraint be added (e.g., LAeq over any 15-minute window ≤ 85 dB, or LCpeak ≤ 135 dB) to prevent intermittent damaging exposures that comply on average?
